The `.section-${i - 1} span { color: ... }` rule is placed in the general CSS block without a `[data-look="neo"]` selector guard. This means the rule fires for classic and handDrawn looks too, silently changing span label colours for all mindmap renders — not just neo ones. If this is intentional (e.g., fixing HTML label colours across all looks), it should be called out explicitly in the PR description and reviewed independently; otherwise scope it to neo:
```css
[data-look="neo"] .section-${i - 1} span {
  color: ${options['cScaleLabel' + i]};
}
```

Inside `genGradient`, the rule `.section-${i - 1} line { stroke-width: 0; }` has **no** `[data-look="neo"]` selector. `genGradient` is invoked whenever `options.useGradient` is truthy — regardless of the active look. This means that for any gradient-enabled theme used with classic or handDrawn look, all `.section-*` line elements have their stroke width suppressed to zero, hiding lines that should be visible. Scope the rule:
```css
[data-look="neo"] .section-${i - 1} line {
  stroke-width: 0;
}
```

The project convention requires `assignWithDepth` (or `cleanAndMerge`) rather than a shallow object spread when merging config/theme objects, to avoid silent shared-reference mutations. Although `themeVariables` is effectively flat in practice, using the spread operator `{ ...config.themeVariables, ... }` deviates from the codebase-wide pattern. Prefer:
```typescript
import { assignWithDepth } from './utils.js';
// ...
assignWithDepth({ theme: config.theme, look: config.look }, config.themeVariables)
```
